#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Google Antigravity, as an agent thinks with it.

Everything about this brain is in this file and nowhere else. A flag, a conversation id, the shape
of one line of its stream, the fact that its terminal usage block is a running total on a resumed
conversation — none of it appears anywhere under `src/rundesk/`, nothing there imports this, and
nothing here imports rundesk. The seam is a pipe carrying newline-delimited records, and it is what
lets a product that names no vendor reach one.

**rundesk runs the vendor's own published CLI, as the owner, through the interface the vendor
documents.** A prompt piped into `agy --output-format stream-json` is that interface: piping selects
print mode by itself and the turn comes back as NDJSON. No credential is read, copied or moved —
`agy` looks the owner's login up in the machine's own keyring, and this never learns how that
worked. There is no token variable to set, no service-account mode, and no supported
configuration-home override, which is also why this adapter offers **no account commands and no
aliases**: a second account cannot be isolated, so claiming one would be claiming containment that
does not exist.

Two invocations, sharing almost nothing:

    --capabilities   what this can do — offline, no account, the same answer every time
    (no argument)    one turn: the prompt arrives on stdin, records come back on stdout

## What was measured rather than assumed

The tool-stream readings below were probed against `agy 1.1.8`; fresh and resumed conversation
streams were then captured through this adapter at **1.1.13**. Their sanitized records stand in
`tests/samples/antigravity-*.jsonl`, and `cli-versions.lock` names the exact version behind each.

- **The prompt is never an argument.** `-p` takes the next word as the prompt, so using it as a
  switch either leaks the prompt into every process list on the machine or swallows the flag behind
  it. Piping alone selects print mode, and the prompt stays on stdin where nobody else can read it.

- **A fresh turn needs `--new-project`.** Without it an unseen working directory can keep the
  stream's stated `init.cwd` while the tools actually stand in Antigravity's persistent scratch
  project — measured, and it is silent: the stream names the directory that was asked for.

- **`result.usage` is a running total on a resumed conversation**, and the per-step usage blocks are
  this invocation's deltas. So the turn's bill is the sum of the step blocks, and the terminal block
  is used **only** as a fallback on a fresh turn that reported no step usage at all. Adding the
  terminal block to a resumed turn would over-report every conversation that was carried on. On the
  captured fresh turn the step blocks sum to exactly the terminal block — 3,390 input, 380 output,
  16,274 cache reads — which is what makes the fallback safe there and unsafe on a resume.

- **The last parent `agent_response` is a level, not a quantity.** Its own input and cache-read
  pieces are the prompt that one response was sent, which is how big the conversation had become. It
  goes *down* after a compaction, so it is assigned and never added — and a response belonging to a
  child conversation is a subagent's own size, so it may not replace the parent's.

- **An unknown conversation id does not fail.** Asked to carry on one it no longer has, 1.1.8
  silently mints a new one and may stand it in the scratch project. The only evidence is that `init`
  names a conversation nobody asked for, and it arrives before the brain has done anything — which
  is what makes stopping there and starting again cleanly possible. See `one_turn`.

- **A soft denial can exit zero saying `SUCCESS` with an empty response.** A tool that failed, no
  answer at all, and a terminal status claiming success is not a turn that worked. That is the one
  place this contradicts the vendor's own word, and it is deliberately narrow: a tool error followed
  by a real answer is a successful turn, because recovering from a failed tool is ordinary work.

- **The line that ends a turn ends it once.** Rundesk settles a turn on the `done` it reads, so a
  brain that said `SUCCESS` and then went on talking must not be able to take back a status, a
  failure message, a conversation handle or a bill the owner has already been given. Nothing after
  the first terminal line crosses the seam; all of it is still kept verbatim, because that drift is
  the only news in it.

## What this deliberately does not report

**No `model_name`, on either version.** `model` is answered *false*, and an adapter that declares no
and then names one anyway has settled the turn's ledger with the one thing it said it had no
evidence for — rundesk keeps that column as the model that **answered**. 1.1.8 opens with the slug
it was configured to use and 1.1.13 opens with none at all; a requested route is not proof of the
route that served the turn, and `RUNDESK_MODEL` is still passed on.

**No `file` records.** This brain names files it read and wrote; nothing in its stream says *this
one was made for a person*, which is what a `file` record means and what gets attached to a message
on a channel. Guessing here would mail somebody their own repository.

**No `final` on any `text`.** Its reply arrives as one run of deltas with no phase in it, and
`result.response` is that whole run restated — commentary before a tool included. So this brain
offers no evidence of which part was the answer, and rundesk's own fallback (the last thought after
the last tool) is a better reading than a mark this adapter would have to invent.

**No `failure_code` off the vendor's prose.** Only `SUCCESS` was ever measured, so there is no
vocabulary here to map. A word is written only where **this adapter** is what went wrong — a brain
that would not start, a stream that stopped mid-turn — because those are the failures it can
classify correctly. Everything else keeps its message and leaves the column empty rather than
filling it with a guess.

## Access, and what it is not

`RUNDESK_ACCESS_MODE` is a **request**, and rundesk enforces nothing. `read` is mapped onto this
brain's own `plan` workflow, which is what the word means to it; `work` is mapped onto
`accept-edits`. Neither is containment and neither is claimed as any: `plan` is a workflow mode, and
a turn under it still runs with the whole machine available.

`--sandbox` is **not** passed, and that is a decision rather than an oversight. It is the vendor's OS
containment, its effect on a turn that must run this install's own `rundesk` command and write the
files the agent lives by is unprobed at 1.1.13, and passing it would imply a boundary rundesk does
not enforce and could not honour.

`--dangerously-skip-permissions` **is** passed, on both access modes, and this is the explicit
decision the flag's name asks for. Print mode has no channel through which an approval could be
asked or given, so without it a turn does not become safer — it becomes a soft denial that exits
zero claiming success, which is the failure this adapter has to correct after the fact instead.
"""

import contextlib
import json
import os
import subprocess
import sys
import threading

#: The program. Found by name on `PATH`, exactly as a person runs it.
AGY = "agy"

#: The surface. Streaming, so records arrive while the turn runs rather than in one lump at the end.
STREAMING = ("--output-format", "stream-json")

#: How long `agy --version` may take when `--capabilities` is asked. A local exec with no network in
#: it; rundesk allows the whole answer 60 seconds, and this leaves room for the rest of it.
VERSION_WITHIN = 10.0

#: How long print mode may wait, in this brain's own words. **Its default is five minutes**, which
#: would cut short every turn that takes longer than one, so the vendor's clock is set past the
#: point where rundesk's own bounds — a silence window of half an hour and a ceiling of 48 hours —
#: are the ones that decide. Two clocks disagreeing is how a turn dies with nothing written down.
#:
#: **Spelled the way this brain spells its own default.** `agy --help` prints `5m0s`, so `48h0m0s`
#: is the same formatter's output for the ceiling rather than a shape somebody hoped it would take —
#: which matters because the value could not be proved to parse without running a turn. If it is
#: ever rejected, the brain exits at once saying so and the turn ends `crashed` carrying its words,
#: which is loud rather than silent.
PRINT_TIMEOUT = "48h0m0s"

#: How long to wait for a thread to finish once the turn is over, and for the brain to end.
GOING_WITHIN = 5.0

#: How much of one line is read at once. Not a limit on what the brain may say — only on what is
#: held while it is being read, which is what makes the bound true rather than aspirational.
A_LINE_AT_MOST = 1024 * 1024

#: How much of what a tool came to goes in its `result` summary, and how many of the brain's last
#: complaints are kept to explain a turn that stopped without saying why.
A_SUMMARY_AT_MOST = 400
TROUBLE_KEPT = 20

#: What a tool this brain offers actually did, in rundesk's words. Deliberately not a guess at the
#: whole list: an unknown tool still gets a `tool` record with **no** `did`, so a tool added next
#: release is never handed a meaning somebody invented for it.
#:
#: **A name is kept once it has been read off this brain, and taken out only on evidence.** A tool
#: missing from one release's listing is not a tool that is gone — the older stream still names it,
#: and dropping it would silently stop reporting an act rundesk has a word for. So this grows with
#: what 1.1.13 offers and loses nothing 1.1.8 proved.
DID_FOR = {
    "view_file": "read", "sed_file": "read", "read_resource": "read",
    "read_url_content": "read", "read_browser_page": "read",
    "list_dir": "list", "list_resources": "list",
    "list_browser_pages": "list", "list_permissions": "list",
    "grep_search": "search", "code_search": "search", "search_web": "search",
    "find_by_name": "search",
    "run_command": "run", "command_status": "run", "send_command_input": "run",
    "notebook_execution": "run", "execute_browser_javascript": "run",
    "replace_file_content": "edit", "multi_replace_file_content": "edit",
    "write_to_file": "edit", "notebook_edit": "edit",
    "generate_image": "make",
    "define_subagent": "delegate", "invoke_subagent": "delegate", "browser_subagent": "delegate",
}

#: Which of a tool's parameters names the file it changed. Both spellings were measured —
#: `AbsolutePath` at 1.1.8 and `TargetFile` at 1.1.13. A spelling this has never seen leaves an edit
#: reported as the plain `edit` it is, which is the safe direction: saying an agent rewrote what it
#: lives by when it did not is worse than not saying it did when it has.
EDITED_AT = ("AbsolutePath", "TargetFile")

#: The steps this brain sends that are framing or state rather than something that happened:
#: `user_input`, `checkpoint`, `system_message`, `error_message`, `unknown`. They produce no record
#: — but a `checkpoint` carries usage, and that usage is part of the turn's bill.

#: What the seam owns. Settings may hand this brain a vendor option that shipped after this release
#: did, but a flag that decides where the prompt goes, which conversation is answered, or what
#: access a turn has is this adapter's and cannot be taken over from outside it.
OWNED_FLAGS = {
    "-p", "--print", "--prompt", "-i", "--prompt-interactive",
    "--output-format", "--print-timeout", "--json-schema", "--log-file",
    "--conversation", "--continue", "-c", "--new-project", "--project",
    "--mode", "--sandbox", "--dangerously-skip-permissions", "--model",
}

#: Which of those take their value as the following word, so ignoring the flag also ignores its
#: value. A boolean has no following word and must not eat the next flag somebody meant to pass.
OWNED_FLAGS_WITH_A_VALUE = {
    "-p", "--print", "--prompt", "-i", "--prompt-interactive",
    "--output-format", "--print-timeout", "--json-schema", "--log-file",
    "--conversation", "--project", "--mode", "--model",
}


def main() -> int:
    if "--capabilities" in sys.argv[1:]:
        say(what_this_can_do())
        return 0
    return one_turn()


# ── What this can do ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

def what_this_can_do() -> dict:
    """Asked offline, with no account and no network, and the same answer every time.

    `steer` is *false*, and it is the only one of the five that changes how a turn is run: there is
    no documented headless channel that accepts a word after the piped prompt, so declaring it would
    leave rundesk holding this adapter's input open for a brain that will never read another line.

    `account_aliases` is absent for the same kind of reason. This brain authenticates through the
    machine's own keyring with no supported configuration-home override, so a second account could
    not be kept apart from the first — and an alias that quietly shared one login is worse than none.

    The version is volunteered beside the five: rundesk keeps whatever an adapter says here against
    every turn, so a vendor upgrade that changes behaviour is visible in the record afterwards
    rather than being a thing somebody has to remember. `model` is false because 1.1.13 accepts a
    requested slug but does not name the model that actually answered in its stream.
    """
    # 1.1.13 no longer names the model that actually answered in its headless stream. The
    # requested slug is not proof of backend routing, so model reporting stays false even though
    # RUNDESK_MODEL may still select one.
    can = {"tools": True, "resume": True, "model": False, "usage": True, "steer": False}
    version = the_version()
    if version:
        can["antigravity_cli"] = version
    return can


def the_version() -> str:
    """What `agy --version` says, or nothing. **Never a reason to fail** — a version this could not
    read does not change what the adapter can do.

    **Started with the same environment a turn is**, which is the whole of why it is worth saying:
    this is the one place `--capabilities` runs the vendor, and a brain that took the question as an
    invitation to fetch a new release would turn an answer that is meant to be offline and the same
    every time into a download — and would land that release in the middle of the turn admitted
    moments later. Nothing wider than the adapter's own environment is handed over, because that
    environment is what rundesk built for this seam and nothing here may reach past it.
    """
    try:
        got = subprocess.run([AGY, "--version"], capture_output=True, text=True,
                             stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, timeout=VERSION_WITHIN, check=False,
                             env=the_environment())
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        return ""
    return got.stdout.strip().splitlines()[0] if got.stdout.strip() else ""


# ── What a turn is started with ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

def opening(access: str, model, resume, settings: dict) -> list:
    """Everything after the program's name, and why each part of it is there.

    Pure and side-effect free, so the whole command line is readable without starting anything —
    which is also what lets a case prove the prompt is not on it.

    The log file is sent to the null device. This brain writes its own diagnostics wherever it
    likes by default, and a turn standing in the agent's own home would otherwise leave one there
    for the agent to find and wonder about.
    """
    argv = [*STREAMING, "--print-timeout", PRINT_TIMEOUT, "--log-file", os.devnull]
    argv += passed_through(settings)

    if resume:
        argv += ["--conversation", str(resume)]
    else:
        argv += ["--new-project"]

    if model:
        argv += ["--model", str(model)]

    # A request, never containment — see the module docstring. `plan` is this brain's own read-only
    # workflow, which is the nearest true thing the word `read` maps onto here.
    argv += ["--mode", "plan" if access == "read" else "accept-edits"]
    argv += ["--dangerously-skip-permissions"]
    return argv


def what_the_owner_set() -> dict:
    """Whatever the owner keeps for this brain, as an object, or nothing at all."""
    try:
        given = json.loads(os.environ.get("RUNDESK_SETTINGS") or "{}")
    except ValueError:
        went_wrong("what was set for this brain is not readable JSON, so none of it was used")
        return {}
    if not isinstance(given, dict):
        went_wrong("what was set for this brain is not a JSON object, so none of it was used")
        return {}
    return given


def passed_through(settings: dict) -> list:
    """Vendor flags the owner asked for, minus the ones this adapter owns.

    Each stays its own word, because a value joined onto its flag is a value the brain reads as part
    of the flag's name. A flag this adapter owns is dropped **with its value**, so ignoring
    `--mode plan` does not leave a stray `plan` behind to be read as something else entirely.
    """
    given = settings.get("flags")
    if given is None:
        return []
    if not isinstance(given, list) or not all(isinstance(one, str) for one in given):
        went_wrong("`flags` is passed to this brain as a list of strings; it was not, so it was "
                   "ignored")
        return []

    kept, skipping = [], False
    for one in given:
        if skipping:
            skipping = False
            continue
        name = one.split("=", 1)[0]
        if name in OWNED_FLAGS:
            went_wrong(f"`{name}` is decided by rundesk for this brain and was ignored")
            skipping = "=" not in one and name in OWNED_FLAGS_WITH_A_VALUE
            continue
        kept.append(one)
    return kept


def the_prompt(asked: str) -> str:
    """What rundesk wants said before the brain reads a word of the task, and then the task.

    **Marked, and never mixed into the person's words.** This brain has no flag that appends to its
    own instructions, so the preface travels in the prompt — which makes saying where it ends the
    only thing that keeps the two apart.
    """
    preface = (os.environ.get("RUNDESK_PREFACE") or "").strip()
    if not preface:
        return asked
    return f"<rundesk-standing-instructions>\n{preface}\n</rundesk-standing-instructions>\n\n{asked}"


def the_environment() -> dict:
    """What the brain is started with, on both invocations — a turn, and the version probe.

    **`USER` is put back, and it is not cosmetic.** This brain looks the owner's login up in the
    machine's keyring under that name, and rundesk builds an adapter's environment from a named
    handful that does not include it — so without this, a signed-in machine can report itself signed
    out and every turn fails telling the owner to run a login they have already run.

    **Nothing here reads, copies, moves or exports a credential.** The only thing added besides the
    account name is the request that this brain not update itself in the middle of somebody's turn.
    """
    env = os.environ.copy()
    with contextlib.suppress(Exception):
        import getpass
        env.setdefault("USER", getpass.getuser())
    env["AGY_CLI_DISABLE_AUTO_UPDATE"] = "true"
    return env


# ── One turn ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

def one_turn() -> int:
    """One turn, however many times the brain has to be started to take it.

    **A turn always ends with a `done`**, whatever went wrong, because rundesk reading no `done` at
    all is a turn nobody can explain. The one exception is a process killed outright, which is
    rundesk's own to classify and the only case it can classify correctly.

    A conversation this brain no longer has is the one failure worth retrying here, and it has to be
    retried by starting again: asked to carry on an id it does not know, this brain does not fail —
    it silently mints another one and may stand it in its own scratch project. The evidence arrives
    on the first line, before the model has done anything, so the first attempt is stopped there and
    **nothing it said is passed on**. That is what makes the retry free of a second helping of
    whatever the first attempt had already begun.
    """
    where = os.environ.get("RUNDESK_CWD") or os.getcwd()
    access = os.environ.get("RUNDESK_ACCESS_MODE")
    if access not in ("read", "work"):
        finished(False, failure_code="crashed",
                 failure_message="rundesk sent an unknown access mode")
        return 1

    asked = sys.stdin.read()
    if not asked.strip():
        finished(False, failure_code="crashed", failure_message="rundesk sent no prompt")
        return 1
    asked = the_prompt(asked)

    settings = what_the_owner_set()
    model = os.environ.get("RUNDESK_MODEL")
    resume = os.environ.get("RUNDESK_RESUME") or None

    turn = Turn(where, asked, [AGY, *opening(access, model, resume, settings)], resume)
    code = turn.run()
    if not turn.the_conversation_was_gone:
        return code
    went_wrong(f"antigravity no longer has conversation {resume}; starting a new one and asking "
               "again")
    again = Turn(where, asked, [AGY, *opening(access, model, None, settings)], None)
    return again.run()


class Turn:
    """One attempt at one turn: one brain started, read, and ended."""

    def __init__(self, where: str, asked: str, argv: list, resume):
        self.where = where
        self.asked = asked
        self.argv = argv
        self.resume = resume
        self.brain = None
        self.raw = kept_at(os.environ.get("RUNDESK_RAW"))
        self.trouble = []

        #: Whether the one record that ends a turn has been said. Rundesk reading none at all is a
        #: turn nobody can explain, and reading two is a turn it cannot reconcile.
        self.said_done = False

        #: Whether this brain has been heard from since. Said once rather than per line, because
        #: what follows a terminal record may be a whole second turn's worth of them.
        self.said_more_than_a_turn = False

        #: How many lines were too long to hold and went whole. Counted so that the gap said about
        #: the second one is not mistaken for a second telling of the first.
        self.gaps = 0

        #: Whether this brain answered a conversation nobody asked for, which is the one and only
        #: shape a lost conversation has here. Read on the first line and acted on immediately.
        self.the_conversation_was_gone = False

        #: What the stream said, gathered as it goes. `ended` is whether the terminal line arrived
        #: at all — a stream that simply stopped is a turn this side has to end itself.
        self.session = None
        self.ended = False
        self.said_something = False
        self.a_tool_failed = None

        #: The bill, summed from the per-step blocks, and whether any arrived. Zero measured and
        #: nothing measured are different answers, and only the second leaves the field out.
        self.counted = {}
        self.counted_anything = False

        #: How big the conversation had become, off the last parent response. Assigned, never added.
        self.context_tokens = None

    def run(self) -> int:
        try:
            self.brain = subprocess.Popen(
                self.argv, stdin=subprocess.PIPE, stdout=subprocess.PIPE,
                stderr=subprocess.PIPE, text=True, bufsize=1, cwd=self.where,
                env=the_environment(),
                # **`errors="replace"`, and it is not tidiness.** A coding agent reads binary files
                # and what a tool printed comes back inside the stream, so one stray byte would be a
                # `UnicodeDecodeError` raised inside the read, past every guard around a record.
                errors="replace")
        except OSError as gone:
            went_wrong(f"the Antigravity CLI would not start: {gone}")
            self.could_not_go_on(f"the Antigravity CLI would not start: {gone}")
            return 1

        noticing = threading.Thread(target=self.noticed, name="agy-err", daemon=True)
        noticing.start()
        try:
            self.the_prompt_goes_in()
            self.heard()
        except Exception as why:                       # noqa: BLE001 — a turn that dies silently is
            # a turn rundesk can only call `crashed` with nothing at all to read. Whatever this was,
            # it is said, and said through the same claim every other ending goes through.
            self.could_not_go_on(f"the antigravity adapter went wrong: {type(why).__name__}: {why}")
            return 1
        finally:
            self.take_the_brain_away(noticing)

        if self.the_conversation_was_gone:
            return 1
        if not self.ended:
            self.could_not_go_on(why_it_stopped(self.trouble, self.brain.returncode))
            return 1
        return 0

    def the_prompt_goes_in(self) -> None:
        """The prompt, and then nothing more. **The input is closed after it**, because this brain
        cannot be steered: it reads to the end of what it was given and starts, so an input held
        open is a brain waiting for an end that is not coming."""
        with contextlib.suppress(OSError, ValueError):
            self.brain.stdin.write(self.asked)
            self.brain.stdin.close()

    def heard(self) -> None:
        """Every line the brain says, for as long as it says anything.

        **Written to the raw stream before it is read**, so a line this release cannot understand is
        still on disk to compare against when somebody asks why records went missing.
        """
        for line in self.read_a_line():
            line = line.rstrip("\n")
            if self.raw is not None:
                with contextlib.suppress(OSError):
                    self.raw.write(line + "\n")
                    self.raw.flush()
            for record in self.a_line(line):
                say(record)
            if self.the_conversation_was_gone:
                # Stop before the model does anything in a conversation nobody asked for. Nothing
                # has been said yet — `init` is the first line — so the retry starts from nothing.
                with contextlib.suppress(Exception):
                    self.brain.terminate()
                return

    def read_a_line(self):
        """Every line the brain sends, **bounded**, and never half of one.

        `for line in stdout` is `readline()` with no size, which pulls until it meets a newline — so
        a check on how long a line is happens only once the whole of it is held.

        **Half a record is not a smaller record, it is a corrupt one**, and nothing downstream could
        tell that apart from the brain talking nonsense, so an over-long line is read to its end and
        dropped whole.
        """
        while True:
            said = self.brain.stdout.readline(A_LINE_AT_MOST + 1)
            if not said:
                return
            if not said.endswith("\n"):
                if len(said) > A_LINE_AT_MOST:
                    self.thrown_away()
                    continue
                return
            yield said

    def thrown_away(self) -> None:
        """Read the rest of a line already too long, keep none of it, and **say that it happened**.

        Bounded on every call, for the reason the caller is: what is being skipped past is by
        definition a stream writing without ever ending a line.

        This is the only place this adapter loses something the brain really said, and it is not
        rundesk's own drift counters that would catch it — those count what crossed the seam, and
        this never reaches it. Nor is it kept verbatim: writing the megabyte out again is the same
        megabyte held somewhere else, and half a record is not a smaller record. So what stands in
        its place is one bounded line in the conversation's own error log, which is where somebody
        asking why a stream went quiet will find that it did not.
        """
        self.gaps += 1
        went_wrong(f"antigravity sent a line too long to hold (over {A_LINE_AT_MOST} bytes); "
                   f"it was discarded whole and is gap {self.gaps} of this turn")
        while True:
            more = self.brain.stdout.readline(A_LINE_AT_MOST + 1)
            if not more or more.endswith("\n"):
                return

    def noticed(self) -> None:
        """Everything the brain says went wrong, passed straight on and the tail of it kept.

        **Drained for the brain's whole life, in its own thread.** A second pipe with nothing reading
        it fills its buffer and stops the program dead, which presents half an hour later as a brain
        that simply went silent and sends whoever is looking anywhere but here.
        """
        with contextlib.suppress(Exception):
            for line in self.brain.stderr:
                went_wrong(line.rstrip())
                self.trouble.append(line.strip())
                del self.trouble[:-TROUBLE_KEPT]

    def take_the_brain_away(self, noticing) -> None:
        """End the brain, whatever happened. A brain left running is a brain nobody will reap."""
        if self.brain is not None:
            with contextlib.suppress(Exception):
                if self.brain.stdin and not self.brain.stdin.closed:
                    self.brain.stdin.close()
            with contextlib.suppress(Exception):
                self.brain.wait(timeout=GOING_WITHIN)
            with contextlib.suppress(Exception):
                self.brain.terminate()
                self.brain.wait(timeout=GOING_WITHIN)
            with contextlib.suppress(Exception):
                self.brain.kill()
            with contextlib.suppress(Exception):
                self.brain.stdout.close()
        with contextlib.suppress(Exception):
            noticing.join(timeout=GOING_WITHIN)
        if self.raw is not None:
            with contextlib.suppress(OSError):
                self.raw.close()

    # ── what the brain said ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    def a_line(self, line: str) -> list:
        """One line of this brain's stream, as the records rundesk understands — or as none.

        Everything not named here is dropped deliberately and kept in the raw stream, which is what
        keeps a vendor adding a line kind from breaking a turn.

        **Nothing crosses the seam once the turn has ended.** Rundesk settles a turn on the `done`
        it reads and a second one is a turn it cannot reconcile — so a brain that keeps talking
        afterwards cannot undo a status, a message, a handle or a bill the owner has already been
        given. What it said is still kept verbatim, because the drift is the news.
        """
        if self.said_done:
            if not self.said_more_than_a_turn:
                self.said_more_than_a_turn = True
                went_wrong("antigravity said more after the line that ended the turn; the first "
                           "ending stands and the rest is kept only in the raw stream")
            return []
        try:
            event = json.loads(line)
        except ValueError:
            return []
        if not isinstance(event, dict):
            return []
        which = event.get("event")
        if which == "init":
            return self.it_started(event)
        if which == "step_update":
            return self.a_step(an_object(event.get("step_update")))
        if which == "result":
            return self.that_is_the_turn(an_object(event.get("result")))
        return []

    def it_started(self, event: dict) -> list:
        """The first line, which says which conversation this is.

        **This is where a lost conversation is caught.** Asked to carry one on, this brain answers
        with the id it actually opened — and when that is not the one that was asked for, it has
        quietly started somewhere else. Nothing has happened yet, so stopping here costs nothing.

        **What it opened with is not read as the model that answered.** 1.1.8 names here the slug it
        was configured to use and 1.1.13 names none at all; neither is this brain saying which
        backend served the turn. See the module docstring.
        """
        session = str(event.get("conversation_id") or "")
        self.session = session or self.session
        if self.resume and session and session != str(self.resume):
            self.the_conversation_was_gone = True
        return []

    def a_step(self, step: dict) -> list:
        """One thing that happened, as what it was.

        Usage is taken off **every** step that carries it, including the framing ones: a checkpoint
        carries part of the turn's bill, and on the captured turn the steps sum to exactly the
        terminal total. What produces no record — `user_input`, `checkpoint`, `system_message`,
        `error_message`, `unknown` — is framing or state, and inventing a record for it would put
        rundesk's own scaffolding in front of somebody as though the agent had done it.
        """
        self.that_cost(step.get("usage"))
        kind = step.get("step_type")
        if kind == "agent_response":
            return self.what_it_said(step)
        if kind == "tool":
            return self.a_tool(step)
        return []

    def what_it_said(self, step: dict) -> list:
        """A piece of what the agent is saying, and — on a finished one — how big the conversation
        had become.

        **A fragment, never marked whole.** This brain writes its reply a delta at a time and
        rundesk gathers them, so claiming each piece was a finished thought would publish half a
        sentence to whoever is watching.
        """
        counted = step.get("usage")
        if step.get("state") == "DONE" and isinstance(counted, dict) and self.is_the_parents(step):
            pieces = [counted.get(one) for one in ("input_tokens", "cache_read_tokens")]
            if any(a_count(one) for one in pieces):
                # A level, not a quantity: **assigned**, so a conversation that was compacted is
                # reported smaller than the one before it rather than larger by the sum of both.
                self.context_tokens = sum(one for one in pieces if a_count(one))
        text = step.get("text_delta")
        if isinstance(text, str) and text:
            self.said_something = True
            return [{"type": "text", "text": text}]
        return []

    def is_the_parents(self, step: dict) -> bool:
        """Whether this response belongs to the conversation this turn is, rather than to a
        subagent's own. A child's size is not this conversation's size, and it can be very much
        larger — so letting one replace the parent's would report a level nothing measured."""
        belongs_to = str(step.get("conversation_id") or "")
        parent = str(self.session or "")
        return not belongs_to or not parent or belongs_to == parent

    def a_tool(self, step: dict) -> list:
        """A tool starting, and what it came to, paired by the index this brain gives them both."""
        info = an_object(step.get("tool_info"))
        name = str(step.get("tool_name") or info.get("name") or "")
        which = "agy:" + str(step.get("step_index", ""))
        state = step.get("state")
        if state == "ACTIVE":
            record = {"type": "tool", "id": which, "name": name}
            did = DID_FOR.get(name)
            if did == "edit":
                did = what_it_changed(info.get("parameters")) or did
            if did:
                record["did"] = did
            return [record]
        if state in ("DONE", "ERROR"):
            worked = state == "DONE"
            summary = f"{name or 'the tool'} finished" if worked else how_it_failed(info)
            if not worked:
                self.a_tool_failed = summary
            return [{"type": "result", "id": which, "ok": worked, "summary": summary}]
        return []

    def that_is_the_turn(self, result: dict) -> list:
        """The line that ends a turn: what it came to, what it cost, and whether it worked."""
        self.ended = True
        self.session = str(result.get("conversation_id") or self.session or "")
        status = str(result.get("status") or "")
        response = str(result.get("response") or "")

        out = []
        # Normally the whole of this already arrived as deltas. Kept as a fallback for the paths
        # that do not stream them, and never as a second copy of what was already said.
        if response and not self.said_something:
            self.said_something = True
            out.append({"type": "text", "text": response, "whole": True})

        # **Only on a fresh turn.** On a resumed one this block is the conversation's running total,
        # so using it as a fallback would bill the whole history against this one turn.
        if not self.counted_anything and not self.resume:
            self.that_cost(result.get("usage"))
        counted = self.the_bill()
        if counted:
            out.append(counted)

        worked = status == "SUCCESS"
        if worked and self.a_tool_failed and not self.said_something:
            # Measured at 1.1.8: a headless soft denial ends `SUCCESS` with an empty response. A
            # tool that failed and no answer at all is not a turn that worked, whatever it says.
            worked = False
        out.append(self.the_ending(worked, result, status))
        self.said_done = True
        return out

    def the_ending(self, worked: bool, result: dict, status: str) -> dict:
        """The record that ends a turn.

        The handle is carried whether or not the turn worked, because this brain **reports** the
        conversation it opened rather than being told one: it exists, and carrying on the one a
        failed turn began is how the next turn keeps what came before it.

        **No `failure_code`**, because nothing in this brain's terminal line classifies a failure
        and a word guessed from prose is wrong on the first release that rewords one. The message is
        kept either way, which is the part a person acts on.
        """
        ending = {"type": "done", "ok": worked}
        if self.session:
            ending["session_id"] = self.session
        if not worked:
            ending["failure_message"] = one_line(
                result.get("error") or self.a_tool_failed or status or "the turn failed")
        return ending

    def that_cost(self, counted) -> None:
        """Add one block to the turn's bill. **Only what was actually measured** — a quantity this
        brain does not report stays absent rather than being summed into a zero that says it cost
        nothing."""
        if not isinstance(counted, dict):
            return
        for ours, theirs in (("input_tokens", "input_tokens"),
                             ("output_tokens", "output_tokens"),
                             ("cache_read_tokens", "cache_read_tokens")):
            if a_count(counted.get(theirs)):
                self.counted[ours] = self.counted.get(ours, 0) + counted[theirs]
                self.counted_anything = True

    def the_bill(self):
        """What this turn cost, or nothing at all when nothing was measured.

        **No `model_name`**, because this adapter answers `model: false` and the two may not
        disagree: rundesk keeps that field as the model that *answered*, written at settlement
        precisely because only the brain knows which one really ran.
        """
        record = {"type": "usage", **self.counted}
        if a_count(self.context_tokens):
            record["context_tokens"] = self.context_tokens
        return record if len(record) > 1 else None

    def could_not_go_on(self, said: str) -> None:
        """End the turn because *this side* could not go on. Said as a `done`, because rundesk
        reading none at all is a turn nobody can explain — and said only once."""
        if self.said_done:
            return
        self.said_done = True
        finished(False, failure_code="crashed", failure_message=one_line(said))


# ── Reading one line ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

def an_object(value) -> dict:
    """One nested object, or an empty one. A vendor sending `null` where an object was is a line
    this reads as saying nothing, never one that raises three frames further in."""
    return value if isinstance(value, dict) else {}


def how_it_failed(info: dict) -> str:
    """Why a tool failed, without relaying what it was working on.

    A tool's own error message carries the path, command or URL it failed at, and a `result` summary
    is stored and shown. The kind of failure is the part a person acts on; the rest is theirs.
    """
    error = an_object(info.get("error"))
    kind = str(error.get("type") or "").strip()
    return f"{kind}: the tool failed" if kind else "the tool failed"


def what_it_changed(parameters) -> str:
    """Which of the files an agent lives by this just changed, when that is what it changed.

    What an agent keeps of its own is what it *is* between turns, so one of those being rewritten is
    different news from a working file being rewritten — and both arrive as the same `edit`. Which
    files they are is not this adapter's to know: `RUNDESK_CONTINUITY` names them, so renaming one is
    a change in one place rather than a verb that quietly stops being reported.

    **Matched on where the file stands, never on what it is called.** Every checkout on the machine
    has an `AGENTS.md`, and an agent editing one in a repository has not rewritten its own rules.
    What qualifies is the resolved path standing directly in the agent's own directory.

    **The path itself never leaves this function.** What crosses the seam is one word, which is what
    lets a surface say what changed without being told where anything lives.
    """
    if not isinstance(parameters, dict):
        return ""
    lives_by = what_the_agent_lives_by()
    if not lives_by:
        return ""
    where = os.path.realpath(os.environ.get("RUNDESK_CWD") or os.getcwd())
    for named in EDITED_AT:
        at = parameters.get(named)
        if not isinstance(at, str) or not at.strip():
            continue
        at = os.path.realpath(os.path.join(where, at))
        if os.path.dirname(at) == where:
            return lives_by.get(os.path.basename(at), "")
    return ""


def what_the_agent_lives_by() -> dict:
    """`NAME=verb` pairs, as rundesk names them. Read rather than held, so a file renamed there does
    not silently stop being reported here."""
    said = {}
    for pair in (os.environ.get("RUNDESK_CONTINUITY") or "").split(","):
        name, _, verb = pair.partition("=")
        if name.strip() and verb.strip():
            said[name.strip()] = verb.strip()
    return said


def why_it_stopped(trouble: list, code) -> str:
    """Why a brain that never finished its turn stopped, in the best words there are."""
    for line in reversed(trouble):
        if line.strip():
            return line.strip()
    return f"antigravity stopped without finishing the turn (exit {code})"


def kept_at(raw):
    """Where the brain's own stream is kept, or nothing when it cannot be.

    **Offered, never required**, and that has to be true of the failure as well as of the absence.
    Opened bare, this would raise out of the one place a turn has nothing to say for itself.
    """
    if not raw:
        return None
    try:
        return open(raw, "a", encoding="utf-8")
    except OSError as gone:
        went_wrong(f"what this brain said could not be kept at {raw}: {gone}")
        return None


# ── Saying it ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

def one_line(said) -> str:
    """A thing to put on a line somebody reads: no newlines, and bounded."""
    said = " ".join(str(said).split())
    return said if len(said) <= A_SUMMARY_AT_MOST else said[:A_SUMMARY_AT_MOST - 1] + "…"


def a_count(value) -> bool:
    """Whether this is a number of things. `True` is an `int` to Python and is not a count."""
    return isinstance(value, int) and not isinstance(value, bool)


def finished(ok: bool, session_id=None, failure_code=None, failure_message=None) -> None:
    """The record that ends a turn. Exactly one is said, and one always is."""
    record = {"type": "done", "ok": bool(ok)}
    for name, value in (("session_id", session_id), ("failure_code", failure_code),
                        ("failure_message", failure_message)):
        if value:
            record[name] = value
    say(record)


def say(record: dict) -> None:
    """One record to rundesk, flushed, because a record held in a buffer is a record nobody saw."""
    with contextlib.suppress(OSError, ValueError):
        sys.stdout.write(json.dumps(record) + "\n")
        sys.stdout.flush()


def went_wrong(said: str) -> None:
    """Something for whoever is reading this turn, on the stream that is the adapter's own."""
    with contextlib.suppress(OSError, ValueError):
        sys.stderr.write(said.rstrip() + "\n")
        sys.stderr.flush()


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
